Classifying the species of a penguin using machine learning

For this project, i have chosen the penguin sizes dataset from kaggle, 
accessible here https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/amulyas/penguin-size-dataset/code
This dataset was chosen because i like penguins and the dataset was highly rated and reccomended for
learning and education. It contains data on around 350 penguins and includes the penguins island of 
residence, species, culmen length, culmen depth, flipper length, body mass and sex. The data was
cleaned so any samples with important values missing were removed.

This data was used in a classification problem where the culmen length, culmen depth, flipper length,
 and body mass was used to determine the species of the penguin. 

This code runs in python. The dependencies and versions needed are listed in the dependencies.txt 
file.

The py Q1-folder file contains the first question, where I solved the problem using a traditional,
non neural network method. I did this by using a skikit-learn prebuilt Random Forest Classifier 
(RFC) function. This specific method was chosen because RFC works well for classification problems 
where the data is in categories, like the data from the penguin dataset. 

The py Q2-folder file contains the second question, where the problem was solved using a 
non-traditional neural network method. For this i chose a MultiLayer Perceptron (MLP) and used the 
prebuilt skikit-learn feature. MLP neural networks work well with continuous, tabular data and can 
account for complex, non-linear relationships. The penguin sizes dataset matches this well too.

The py Q3-folder file contains a reserach question I chose from a selection of 5 questions. The 
question I chose and worked with was question choice 5, How do choices about data such as i) amount 
of training data and ii) balance of classes in a classification problem affect the performance of a 
neural network? 
This question was chosen because it is a good fit for my data and would be easy enough to filter the 
data into different size training and testing groups and change the class balances, as these are 
options built in to skikit-learn. I had already made helper functions that would be able to handle 
splitting the data into different sized groups for testing and training that I was able to reuse, so 
after altering and using a premade skikit-learn function to imbalance the classes i was able to 
change the data to complete this question quite easily. 
I felt this question was well suited to my data compared to some of the other options such as data 
augmentation, and it was relatively simple to complete and analyse. 